# Trivy image-scan suppressions for the CI gate (.github/workflows/ci.yml).
# Scope: FIXABLE CRITICAL/HIGH only (CI runs --ignore-unfixed). Remove an entry
# the moment its finding becomes genuinely fixable in our image.
#
# Trivy's legacy ignorefile matches the EXACT VulnerabilityID it reports and does
# NOT resolve CVE<->GHSA aliases. Trivy's DB has been flipping these advisories
# from GHSA to CVE as the primary id, which silently un-suppressed them (that is
# what broke this gate). So where an advisory can surface under either id we list
# BOTH, to stay matched whichever one Trivy reports.

# --- web image: vitest UI arbitrary file read/execute -----------------------
# Requires `vitest --ui` to be explicitly started. The frontend image runs the
# Next.js dev server (`npm run dev`), never the Vitest UI server. Not reachable.
CVE-2026-47429
GHSA-5xrq-8626-4rwp

# --- web image: vite server.fs.deny bypass via Windows alternate paths -------
# Windows-specific (NT UNC / alternate data streams). The image runs on Linux.
CVE-2026-53571
GHSA-fx2h-pf6j-xcff

# --- web image: brace-expansion DoS (unbounded expansion -> OOM) -------------
# The PRODUCTION chain is FIXED, not suppressed: regwatch/frontend/package.json
# overrides `brace-expansion@^5.0.0` to ^5.0.8, moving the only prod path
# (@sentry/nextjs -> @sentry/bundler-plugin-core -> glob -> minimatch) off the
# vulnerable range. `npm audit --omit=dev --audit-level=high` is clean.
#
# The two APP-TREE copies are now FIXED, not suppressed: upstream backported the
# patch to the legacy lines, so package-lock.json pins
# node_modules/brace-expansion@1.1.18 and
# @redocly/openapi-core/node_modules/brace-expansion@2.1.4. Both still do
# `module.exports = expandTop` (verified against the 1.1.18/2.1.4 tarballs), so
# their consumers -- which `require('brace-expansion')` and CALL the result --
# keep working. That is why the 5.x override stays scoped to ^5.0.0: only the 5.x
# line returns {expand} instead of a callable.
#
# What remains cannot be fixed HERE and is dev-tooling only:
#   * usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion@5.0.7 -- npm's
#     OWN bundled copy from `npm install -g npm@11.18.0` in the Dockerfile. No
#     lockfile change reaches it, and npm 11.19.0 (the newest release this
#     node:20 base can take -- npm 12 needs Node >=22) still bundles 5.0.7, so
#     bumping the pin does nothing. It moves when npm ships a rebuilt release.
# The image serves Next.js; npm's bundled copy is not on a request path, and the
# advisory needs attacker-controlled brace input to the expander. Drop entries as
# npm ships fixed releases.
CVE-2026-14257
GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg
# Same package, later advisory: DoS via unbounded intermediate arrays, bypassing
# the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation. Affects npm's bundled 5.0.7 (fixed in 5.0.9).
CVE-2026-69152
GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895

# --- web image: ip-address SSRF via leading-zero octet confusion -------------
# usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ip-address@10.2.0 (fixed 10.3.1),
# pulled in by npm's own socks-proxy-agent. Same story as brace-expansion above:
# it is inside the globally installed npm, no lockfile reaches it, and npm
# 11.19.0 still bundles 10.2.0. Address4 only parses addresses npm itself hands
# it when talking through a SOCKS proxy -- the image sets no proxy and the app
# never calls this code. Drop when npm rebundles.
CVE-2026-69192
GHSA-mwp4-54f8-5fhr

# --- web image: Go stdlib CVEs inside esbuild's bundled Go binary ------------
# esbuild ships as a pre-compiled Go binary (@esbuild/<platform>/bin/esbuild),
# pulled in transitively by vite/vitest as a dev/build dependency. Trivy flags
# the Go stdlib (v1.20.12) baked into that binary. We do not build esbuild and
# cannot bump its embedded Go toolchain; only an upstream esbuild release can.
# esbuild runs as a local code transform, never as a network or TLS peer, so
# these net/* (DoS, IP parsing) and crypto/tls (session-resumption certificate
# validation) issues are not reachable in our usage. Drop entries as newer
# esbuild ships rebuilt binaries.
#
# Verified Jun 2026 by reproducing the CI scan: every CVE below comes from the
# WEB image's esbuild Go binary, NOT a Python dependency in the API image.
# NOTE (Jul 2026): since PR #93 the API image DOES contain a Go binary
# (/usr/local/bin/regwatch-proxy, built from the Dockerfile's digest-pinned
# golang stage), so a Go stdlib CVE can now legitimately fire on the API image
# too. The remedy there is bumping the pinned golang digest and rebuilding --
# never an entry in this file (we own that toolchain, unlike esbuild's).
CVE-2023-45288
CVE-2024-24790
CVE-2024-34156
CVE-2025-61726
CVE-2025-61729
CVE-2025-68121
CVE-2026-25679
CVE-2026-27145
CVE-2026-32280
CVE-2026-32281
CVE-2026-32283
CVE-2026-33811
CVE-2026-33814
CVE-2026-39820
CVE-2026-39823
CVE-2026-39825
CVE-2026-39836
CVE-2026-42499
CVE-2026-42504
